(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_thesblockelements=[{id:"neet-2023-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological importance of Mg and Ca",q:"Which one of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Mg plays roles in neuromuscular function and interneuronal transmission","The daily requirement of Mg and Ca in the human body is estimated to be 0.2 \u2013 0.3 g.","All enzymes that utilise ATP in phosphate transfer require Ca as the cofactor","The bone in human body is an inert and unchanging substance"],c:1,s:`Ca (not Mg) plays the role in neuromuscular function and interneuronal transmission, so option 1 is wrong.
The daily need of Mg and Ca is about 200\u2013300 mg (0.2\u20130.3 g), so option 2 is correct.
Enzymes using ATP for phosphate transfer need Mg as the cofactor, not Ca, so option 3 is wrong.
Bone is not inert; it is continually dissolved and redeposited, so option 4 is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2023-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Alkali metals in liquid ammonia",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : Metallic sodium dissolves in liquid ammonia giving a deep blue solution, which is paramagnetic.
Reason R : The deep blue solution is due to the formation of amide.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:3,s:`Sodium in liquid ammonia gives M + (x + y)NH\u2083 \u2192 [M(NH\u2083)\u2093]\u207A + [e(NH\u2083)\u1D67]\u207B.
The ammoniated electron absorbs visible light, which gives the deep blue colour.
The unpaired electron makes the solution paramagnetic.
So A is true, but R is false because the colour is not due to amide.`},{id:"neet-2022-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Properties of alkali metals",q:"Identify the incorrect statement from the following",o:["The oxidation number of K in KO\u2082 is +4.","Ionisation enthalpy of alkali metals decreases from top to bottom in the group.","Lithium is the strongest reducing agent among the alkali metals.","Alkali metals react with water to form their hydroxides."],c:0,s:`Alkali metals show only the +1 oxidation state in their compounds.
KO\u2082 is a superoxide containing O\u2082\u207B and K\u207A, so K is +1, not +4.`},{id:"neet-2022-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Uses of alkali metals",q:`List-I: (a) Li (b) Na (c) KOH (d) Cs
List-II: (i) absorbent for carbon dioxide (ii) electrochemical cells (iii) coolant in fast breeder reactors (iv) photoelectric cell
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) - (iii), (b) \u2013 (iv), (c) \u2013 (ii), (d) \u2013 (i)","(a) - (i), (b) \u2013 (iii), (c) \u2013 (iv), (d) \u2013 (ii)","(a) - (ii), (b) \u2013 (iii), (c) \u2013 (i), (d) \u2013 (iv)","(a) - (iv), (b) \u2013 (i), (c) \u2013 (iii), (d) \u2013 (ii)"],c:2,s:`Lithium is used in electrochemical cells.
Liquid sodium is a coolant in fast breeder reactors.
KOH absorbs CO\u2082 and forms K\u2082CO\u2083.
Cs has low ionisation enthalpy, so it is used in photoelectric cells.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q074",pn:74,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Bleaching powder",q:"CaCl\u2082 and Ca(OCl)\u2082 are components of :",o:["lime water","gypsum","Portland cement","bleaching powder"],c:3,s:"Bleaching powder is a mixture containing CaCl\u2082 and Ca(OCl)\u2082."},{id:"neet-2021-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Anomalous behaviour of beryllium",q:"The structures of beryllium chloride in solid state and vapour phase, are :",o:["Chain in both","Chain and dimer, respectively","Linear in both","Dimer and Linear, respectively"],c:1,s:`In the solid, each Be is joined to two Cl bridges on each side, giving a polymeric chain.
In the vapour phase it forms a chloro-bridged dimer.
So chain (solid) and dimer (vapour).`,sfig:{w:280,h:185,alt:"Solid BeCl2 as a chain of Be atoms joined by double chlorine bridges, and the vapour as a dimer with two bridging Cl and one terminal Cl on each Be",d:[["t",60,48,"Be","m"],["t",135,48,"Be","m"],["t",210,48,"Be","m"],["t",97,22,"Cl","m"],["t",172,22,"Cl","m"],["t",97,74,"Cl","m"],["t",172,74,"Cl","m"],["l",69,37,88,24],["l",106,24,126,37],["l",69,51,88,61],["l",106,61,126,51],["l",144,37,163,24],["l",181,24,201,37],["l",144,51,163,61],["l",181,61,201,51],["p","M52 37 L15.6 11.4 L12.4 16.6 Z","F"],["p","M52 51 L12.4 71.4 L15.6 76.6 Z","F"],["l",219,37,248,21],["l",219,51,248,67],["t",256,24,"Cl","m"],["t",256,73,"Cl","m"],["t",75,144,"Be","m"],["t",145,144,"Be","m"],["t",110,114,"Cl","m"],["t",110,174,"Cl","m"],["t",18,144,"Cl","m"],["t",202,144,"Cl","m"],["l",29,140,66,140],["l",154,140,191,140],["l",83,134,102,121],["l",118,121,137,134],["l",83,146,102,159],["l",118,159,137,146]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Covalent character of halides",q:"Among the following alkaline earth metal halides, one which is covalent and soluble in organic solvents is :",o:["Beryllium chloride","Calcium chloride","Strontium chloride","Magnesium chloride"],c:0,s:`Be\xB2\u207A is very small, so it polarises Cl\u207B strongly (Fajans' rule) and BeCl\u2082 is covalent.
Covalent compounds dissolve in organic solvents.
The chlorides of Mg, Ca and Sr are ionic.`},{id:"neet-2020-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological importance of Na and K",q:"The following metal ion activates many enzymes, participates in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP and with Na, is responsible for the transmission of nerve signals.",o:["Copper","Calcium","Potassium","Iron"],c:2,s:`Potassium ions activate many enzymes and help in glucose oxidation to give ATP.
With sodium, K\u207A takes part in nerve signal transmission.`},{id:"neet-2019-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Anomalous behaviour of beryllium",q:"Which of the following is an amphoteric hydroxide ?",o:["Mg(OH)\u2082","Be(OH)\u2082","Sr(OH)\u2082","Ca(OH)\u2082"],c:1,s:`Be(OH)\u2082 reacts with both acids and bases, so it is amphoteric.
The hydroxides of Mg, Ca and Sr are basic.`},{id:"neet-2019-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological role of Mg",q:"Enzymes that utilize ATP is phosphate transfer require an alkaline earth metal (M) as the cofactor. M is -",o:["Ca","Sr","Be","Mg"],c:3,s:`Enzymes that move phosphate from ATP need magnesium as a cofactor.
So M is Mg.`},{id:"neet-2018-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Nature of oxides of group 2",q:"Which of the following oxides is most acidic in nature?",o:["MgO","BeO","BaO","CaO"],c:1,s:`Basic strength of the oxide increases as the metal's ionisation energy decreases.
Ionisation energy falls down the group: Be > Mg > Ca > Ba.
So basic nature is BeO < MgO < CaO < BaO.
BeO is the least basic, so the most acidic (actually amphoteric).`},{id:"neet-2016-q025",pn:25,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Calcium carbide with nitrogen",q:"The product obtained as a result of a reaction of nitrogen with CaC\u2082 is",o:["CaCN","CaCN\u2083","Ca\u2082CN","Ca(CN)\u2082"],a:"Bonus",s:`Nitrogen reacts with calcium carbide on heating to give calcium cyanamide.
CaC\u2082 + N\u2082 \u2192 CaCN\u2082 + C
The product CaCN\u2082 is not among the options.
Note: Printed key is Bonus: none of the options is CaCN\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2016-q043",pn:43,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological role of Ca and Mg",q:"Which of the following statements is false ?",o:["Ca\xB2\u207A ions are important blood in clotting","Ca\xB2\u207A ions are not important in maintaining the regular beating of heart","Mg\xB2\u207A ions are important in the green parts of plants","Mg\xB2\u207A ions form a complex with ATP"],c:1,s:`Ca\xB2\u207A is needed for blood clotting, nerve impulse and the regular beating of the heart.
Mg\xB2\u207A is the central atom of chlorophyll and forms a complex with ATP.
So the false statement is (2): Ca\xB2\u207A is important for the heartbeat.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q064",pn:64,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Compounds of calcium",q:"The suspension of slaked lime in water is known as",o:["limewater","quicklime","milk of lime","aqueous solution of slaked lime"],c:2,s:`Slaked lime is Ca(OH)\u2082, made from quicklime CaO and water.
It is sparingly soluble in water.
Its clear aqueous solution is limewater.
A suspension of slaked lime in water is milk of lime.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q068",pn:68,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Properties of beryllium",q:"In context with beryllium, which one of the following statements is incorrect ?",o:["It is rendered passive by nitric acid","It forms Be\u2082C","Its salts rarely hydrolyze","Its hydride is electron-deficient and polymeric"],c:2,s:`Be is made passive by conc. HNO\u2083 (an oxide layer forms), so (1) is true.
Be forms the carbide Be\u2082C, so (2) is true.
BeH\u2082 is electron deficient and polymeric, so (4) is true.
Be\xB2\u207A is small and highly polarising, so its salts hydrolyse readily: BeCl\u2082 + 2H\u2082O \u2192 Be(OH)\u2082 + 2HCl.
So (3) is the incorrect statement.`}]})();
